\description{
Methods for the \code{{rstantools}} posterior-prediction generics. They return a
draws-by-observation matrix and enable \code{{tidybayes}} workflows such as
\code{add_epred_draws()}, \code{add_predicted_draws()}, and \code{add_linpred_draws()}.
mcp exports these generics, so they work without \code{{rstantools}}. If \code{{rstantools}} or
\code{{brms}} masks them, their generics dispatch to the same methods.
}
\details{
For GARMA models, \code{posterior_predict()} conditions on the observed
response history, just like \code{predict()}. Use \code{arma = "replicate"} in either
method to generate fresh response histories recursively. Missing responses
in the original data are filled with retained imputations only to supply
histories. With \code{arma = TRUE}, \code{posterior_predict()} draws from the response
distributions whose means \code{posterior_epred()} returns, including at missing
rows. These methods require posterior draws. For prior prediction, use
\code{predict()} with \code{prior = TRUE}; \code{arma} selects the same behavior.

For binomial models, \code{posterior_epred()} and \code{posterior_predict()} (and
corresponding \code{{tidybayes}} workflows such as \code{add_epred_draws()}) follow
\code{{brms}} and \code{{rstantools}} conventions by returning values on the outcome
count scale (\code{rate = FALSE}), i.e., expected counts \eqn{E[Y] = n\mu} and
simulated counts in \eqn{\{0, \dots, n\}}, matching \code{fitted()} and \code{predict()}.
Use \code{rate = TRUE} for proportions. To obtain the success probability
parameter \eqn{\mu} on the \eqn{[0, 1]} scale regardless of trial counts, pass
\code{dpar = "mu"}. In contrast, \code{posterior_linpred(transform = TRUE)} returns the
inverse-linked parameter directly, evaluating success probabilities \eqn{\mu}
in \eqn{[0, 1]} rather than counts.
}
